  "body": "I am trying to unsuccessfully fix some errors in my code regarding closing ports and I believe it stems from my general confusion with stream locking.\r\n\r\nTo try to get a better handle on things, I am looking at the example from [this page](https://web.dev/serial/):\r\n\r\n```\r\n// Without transform streams.\r\n\r\nconst reader = port.readable.getReader();\r\n\r\n// Listen to data coming from the serial device.\r\nwhile (true) {\r\n  const { value, done } = await reader.read();\r\n  if (done) {\r\n    reader.releaseLock();\r\n    break;\r\n  }\r\n  // value is a Uint8Array.\r\n  console.log(value);\r\n}\r\n\r\n// Force reader.read() to resolve immediately and subsequently\r\n// call reader.releaseLock() in the loop example above.\r\nawait reader.cancel();\r\nawait port.close();\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe line \"await reader.cancel()\" make no sense. According to the comment, it forces the \"reader.read()\" to resolve immediately, but I do not understand this; the only way to reach that line of code is for the \"await reader.read()\" to exit so that the code can break out of the loop, which means the \"reader.read()\" is *already* resolved. So I do not understand what that example is getting at when they call \"reader.cancel()\"\r\n\r\nThat line seems superfluous to me. In general, I do not understand under which situation \"reader.cancel()\" may be useful, unless you are doing some sort of concurrent execution. I assume that if you write a single execution thread using async/await, you should *never* need \"reader.cancel()\". Is this a correct assumption?\r\n\r\n-- Marcio\r\n",
